// Christof_11_1_469           The demon is no more.
// Mendel_11_1_470             The Golem was no demon.
// Christof_11_1_471           It was, and we have sent it straight to hell.
// Mendel_11_1_472             The Golem was animated by the life force, and his spirit now returns to Abraham’s bosom.
// Christof_11_1_473           What? Blaspheming Sorcerer! This monster of clay has no soul!
// Mendel_11_1_474             I am no Sorcerer. I am a Kabbalist. And all life is from God. Every man, animal, and creature. Even this Golem. Even Vampires such as thee.
// Christof_11_1_475           Liar! Thou dost toy with my sanity! One such as I cannot be redeemed. I am an outcast from heaven!
// Mendel_11_1_476             Not so. All life comes from one place, and to that place it must return.
// Christof_11_1_479           Why should God make such creatures?
// Mendel_11_1_480             To vex mankind. They are all part of the Kingdom of God, sent to test our faith, as the Devil did test the simple faith of Job. How hast thy faith endured, Crusader?
// Christof_11_1_481           Not well. For God hath abandoned my soul.
// Mendel_11_1_482             The question is not whether God hath abandoned thee, selfish boy. It’s whether thou wilt abandon God. And become a beast.
// Christof_11_1_487           I shall consider thy words.
// Mendel_11_1_488             Nay, there is no magic in the Shem. ‘Tis merely letters.  Letters which connect the golem to the true Source of power.
// Wilhem_11_1_489             Nonetheless, the Cappadocians would be grateful to get such a thing. It would help them build golems for their own protection.
// Mendel_11_1_490             The Shem is thine, with my blessings, to do with as thou pleaseth.

